ecked\",\n      \"items\": [\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/011\",\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/012\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"No platform-specific package manager, BSD/GNU-only flag or scheduler command in tracked text \\u2014 all a command scan can settle. Whether the documented route needs a mount, a host install, hardware or a service is a reading.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"uri\": \"probe:platform-assumptions\",\n          \"description\": \"scanned 58 tracked paths for 20 platform-specific commands, 0 hits\",\n          \"sha256\": null\n        }\n      ],\n      \"details\": {\n        \"hits\": []\n      },\n      \"item_status\": {\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/011\": \"not-checked\",\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/012\": \"not-checked\"\n      },\n      \"item_reason\": {\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/011\": \"No platform-specific package manager, BSD/GNU-only flag or scheduler command in tracked text \\u2014 all a command scan can settle. Whether the documented route needs a mount, a host install, hardware or a service is a reading.\",\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/012\": \"No platform-specific package manager, BSD/GNU-only flag or scheduler command in tracked text \\u2014 all a command scan can settle. Whether the documented route needs a mount, a host install, hardware or a service is a reading.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"probe\": \"run-env-linked\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"items\": [\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/006\",\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/015\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"must/006 not-checked: No run records, so no record names the environment it ran in. must/015 not-checked: Nothing names an environment to check for a pin.\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"details\": {\n        \"records\": 0,\n        \"with_env\": 0,\n        \"without_env\": [],\n        \"by_mechanism\": {\n          \"container\": 0,\n          \"lockfile\": 0,\n          \"capture\": 0\n        },\n        \"unpinned\": []\n      },\n      \"item_status\": {\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/006\": \"not-checked\",\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/015\": \"not-checked\"\n      },\n      \"item_reason\": {\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/006\": \"No run records, so no record names the environment it ran in.\",\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/015\": \"Nothing names an environment to check for a pin.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"probe\": \"subdatasets-resolvable\",\n      \"status\": \"not-checked\",\n      \"items\": [\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/002\",\n        \"stamped-checklist:may/001\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"No submodules or subdatasets: whether external inputs are linked at all needs a reading of the object.\",\n      \"evidence\": [],\n      \"details\": {\n        \"submodules\": []\n      },\n      \"item_status\": {\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/002\": \"not-checked\",\n        \"stamped-checklist:may/001\": \"not-checked\"\n      },\n      \"item_reason\": {\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/002\": \"No submodules or subdatasets: whether external inputs are linked at all needs a reading of the object.\",\n        \"stamped-checklist:may/001\": \"Inspect independently maintained project components; ordinary runtime/package dependencies are environment evidence. No components makes this item N/A.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"probe\": \"tree-clean\",\n      \"status\": \"unmet\",\n      \"items\": [\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/004\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"4 path(s) are not committed, so the repository is not the whole state: ?? analysis/, ?? figures/, ?? report.md, ?? results/\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"uri\": \"git status --porcelain\",\n          \"description\": \"?? analysis/, ?? figures/, ?? report.md, ?? results/\",\n          \"sha256\": null\n        }\n      ],\n      \"details\": {\n        \"dirty\": [\n          \"?? analysis/\",\n          \"?? figures/\",\n          \"?? report.md\",\n          \"?? results/\"\n        ],\n        \"submodules_modified\": [],\n        \"tracked_text\": 4\n      },\n      \"item_status\": {\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/004\": \"unmet\"\n      },\n      \"item_reason\": {\n        \"stamped-checklist:must/004\": \"4 path(s) are not committed, so the repository is not the whole state: ?? analysis/, ?? figures/, ?? report.md, ?? results/\"\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n# Potential gains and brain activity in the equal-indifference group\n\n## Question and result\n\nAmong the 54 participants in NARPS's equal-indifference group, the supplied gain-effect maps do **not** provide whole-brain family-wise corrected evidence for a gain-related increase in vmPFC. Twenty-three voxels in three small posterior occipital-pole clusters passed the one-sided whole-brain max-statistic permutation test (α = .05; peak t = 6.62 at MNI [-26, -102, -3.6], max-T FWE p = .0006); none were in vmPFC. In an independently defined vmPFC mask, the mean participant effect was **-0.009** (95% t interval **[-0.040, 0.022]**), and no voxel survived whole-brain correction. The occipital finding does not support the vmPFC hypothesis. A lack of vmPFC support is not evidence that the true effect is exactly zero.\n\n![Uncorrected positive whole-brain group t map; inference uses whole-brain max-T correction](figures/whole_brain_t_uncorrected.png)\n\n*Figure 1. Positive-direction one-sample t statistics displayed at uncorrected p < .05 for visualization only. There were 7,326 such voxels; only 23 voxels in posterior occipital-pole clusters passed whole-brain correction.*\n\n![Whole-brain max-T FWE corrected positive gain effects](figures/whole_brain_fwe.png)\n\n*Figure 2. The corrected map shows the small posterior occipital-pole clusters that survive whole-brain FWE; no vmPFC voxels survive.*\n\n![Participant vmPFC estimates](figures/vmPFC_subject_estimates.png)\n\n*Figure 3. Each point is one participant's average effect estimate across voxels in the anatomical vmPFC mask. The interval is a descriptive 95% t interval for the group mean; inferential support is determined by whole-brain correction.*\n\n## Methods\n\nI analyzed all supplied participants as the independent units in a one-sample, positive-direction group t test of participant gain-effect estimates against zero. Input maps were finite, and shared shape and affine were confirmed. The analysis mask was the intersection of voxels with nonzero finite values in all 54 maps and a resampled standard MNI152 brain mask (186,400 voxels); this common-support rule avoids comparing different participant coverage while restricting inference to brain. No participants were excluded. Whole-brain family-wise error was controlled with 10,000 participant-level sign-flip permutations and the maximum t statistic across the complete analysis mask (fixed seed 20261016; minimum attainable p = 1/10,001). Whole-brain correction addresses false positives across locations, at a power cost compared with an ROI-only test.\n\nI localized vmPFC using the Harvard-Oxford cortical atlas (25% maximum-probability map), taking its *Frontal Medial Cortex* label at MNI y ≥ 0 and z ≤ 0 (769 voxels on the input grid). This atlas-defined ventral medial subset is an anatomical aid, not a mask used to select the whole-brain inference. vmPFC boundaries vary across conventions; the anatomical summary is therefore descriptive.\n\n## Interpretation and limits\n\nThese are participant contrast parameter estimates (COPEs), not z/t maps or group statistics. The upstream gain modulator was mean-centered and variance-normalized before HRF convolution, so its coefficient is not response change per currency unit. The response was modeled with response-time durations and separate choice and gain modulators; nonresponses, motion, flagged volumes, drift, and run combination follow the fixed upstream analysis. Gain and loss were estimated in separate models, so the gain coefficient is conditional on that modeling choice. In the task, potential gains varied over twice the range of potential losses, which further cautions against interpreting this coefficient as an isolated monetary-unit slope.\n\nThe one-sample model gives equal weight to each participant because participant variance maps were not supplied. Thus group uncertainty cannot account for differences in first-level precision, and the variance of the COPEs mixes true participant differences with estimation error. Sign-flip inference assumes participant-level errors are symmetric under the null. The data support conclusions only about this selected group, these supplied maps, and this upstream model. Failure to reject does not rule out a smaller positive effect, effects with a different spatial extent, or effects under a different first-level specification; it also does not establish absence of vmPFC involvement.\n\n## Reproducibility\n\nRun `python analysis/group_analysis.py` in the supplied Python environment from this workspace. The script writes group maps, voxelwise p values, QC summaries, and figures. `results/summary.json` contains the key numerical results; `results/participant_qc.tsv` records participant coverage and map summaries. The original study's Hypothesis 1 instructions are available [here](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf).\n# Fixed upstream analysis\n\nEach map is one participant's unthresholded gain-effect estimate from NARPS team AO86, averaged across four runs.\nThe 54 participants comprise the complete equal-indifference group in OpenNeuro ds001734 version 1.0.4.\nThese are effect estimates, not standardized test statistics or group results.\nMaps are reported in MNI152NLin2009cAsym space.\n\nAO86 used fMRIPrep outputs and nistats participant models.\nTheir reported gain regressor was mean-centered and variance-normalized before HRF convolution; its coefficients must not be interpreted as change per currency unit.\nResponse trials used response-time durations, with separate response-choice and gain modulators.\nNonresponse trials, motion terms, flagged volumes and drift terms were also modeled; runs were combined within participant.\nGain and loss used separate models, so the supplied gain effect is conditional on that modeling choice.\n\nThe reported upstream choices are fixed for this task.\nParticipant variance maps and a complete executable upstream pipeline are not supplied; account for these limits when choosing and interpreting group inference.\n`participants.tsv` retains the source group, age and gender columns.\n`sources.json` identifies each original image, its retrieved-byte checksum, the pinned cohort table and the original methods report.\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Group inference for NARPS AO86 equal-indifference participant gain effects.\"\"\"\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport nibabel as nib\nfrom scipy import stats\nfrom scipy.ndimage import label\nfrom nilearn import datasets, image, plotting\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]\nDATA = ROOT / 'input' / 'data'\nOUT = ROOT / 'results'; FIG = ROOT / 'figures'\nOUT.mkdir(exist_ok=True); FIG.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\nN_PERM = 10000\nSEED = 20261016\nALPHA = .05\n\n# Cohort lock: exact participant set from supplied TSV, sorted by identifier.\nmeta = pd.read_csv(ROOT / 'input' / 'participants.tsv', sep='\\t')\npaths = sorted(DATA.glob('*_hypo1_cope.nii.gz'))\nby_id = {p.name.split('_')[0]: p for p in paths}\nids = sorted(meta.participant_id)\nassert len(ids) == 54 and set(ids) == set(by_id) and len(paths) == 54\nimgs = [nib.load(str(by_id[s])) for s in ids]\nref = imgs[0]\nassert all(im.shape == ref.shape and np.allclose(im.affine, ref.affine) for im in imgs)\narrays = np.stack([im.get_fdata(dtype=np.float32) for im in imgs])\nfinite = np.isfinite(arrays).all(axis=0)\n# Supplied effects use zero for outside-brain voxels. Common nonzero support avoids\n# comparing different participant coverage; a standard MNI152 brain mask excludes\n# non-brain edge/background voxels from whole-brain inference.\nnonzero = np.all(arrays != 0, axis=0)\nbrain = image.resample_to_img(datasets.load_mni152_brain_mask(resolution=2), ref,\n                              interpolation='nearest').get_fdata() > .5\nmask = finite & nonzero & brain\nX = arrays[:, mask].astype(np.float64)\nn, v = X.shape\nassert n == 54 and v > 10000\n\n# QC: spatial coverage, global distribution and subject-level summary.\nqc = []\nfor i, sid in enumerate(ids):\n    a = arrays[i]\n    vals = a[mask]\n    qc.append({'participant_id':sid, 'age':int(meta.set_index('participant_id').loc[sid,'age']),\n               'gender':meta.set_index('participant_id').loc[sid,'gender'],\n               'nonzero_voxels':int(np.count_nonzero(a)), 'common_mask_mean':float(vals.mean()),\n               'common_mask_sd':float(vals.std(ddof=1)), 'common_mask_min':float(vals.min()),\n               'common_mask_max':float(vals.max()), 'n_nonfinite':int((~np.isfinite(a)).sum())})\npd.DataFrame(qc).to_csv(OUT/'participant_qc.tsv',sep='\\t',index=False)\n\n# One-sample positive-direction t test. Participants are the independent units.\nmean = X.mean(axis=0)\nsd = X.std(axis=0, ddof=1)\nt_obs = mean / (sd / np.sqrt(n))\ndf = n - 1\np_unc = stats.t.sf(t_obs, df)\n\n# One-sided whole-brain max-T sign-flip permutation test. Sign flips are valid\n# under the null of symmetric participant-level errors; each permutation's maximum\n# t controls family-wise error over every voxel in the common-support mask.\nrng = np.random.default_rng(SEED)\nmax_t = np.empty(N_PERM, dtype=np.float32)\n# Batch multiplication uses optimized BLAS and avoids storing permuted volumes.\nfor start in range(0, N_PERM, 100):\n    b = min(100, N_PERM-start)\n    signs = rng.choice(np.array([-1.,1.]), size=(b,n))\n    sums = signs @ X\n    # sign flipping preserves each participant's squared observation\n    ss = np.sum(X*X, axis=0)[None,:] - (sums*sums)/n\n    var = np.maximum(ss/(n-1), 0)\n    tp = (sums/n) / np.sqrt(var/n)\n    max_t[start:start+b] = np.max(tp, axis=1)\n    del signs, sums, ss, var, tp\nmax_t_sorted = np.sort(max_t)\n# Search each observed t against the sorted permutation maxima to avoid a\n# permutations-by-voxels temporary array.\np_fwe = (1 + (N_PERM - np.searchsorted(max_t_sorted, t_obs, side='left'))) / (N_PERM + 1)\npd.DataFrame({'permutation':np.arange(1,N_PERM+1), 'max_t':max_t}).to_csv(\n    OUT/'permutation_max_t.tsv.gz',sep='\\t',index=False,compression='gzip')\n\n# Save maps on source grid with zero outside common support.\ndef save_map(vals, name, dtype=np.float32):\n    out = np.zeros(ref.shape, dtype=dtype)\n    out[mask] = vals\n    nii = nib.Nifti1Image(out, ref.affine, ref.header)\n    nii.set_data_dtype(dtype)\n    nib.save(nii, OUT/name)\n    return nii\nmean_img = save_map(mean, 'mean_gain_effect.nii.gz')\nt_img = save_map(t_obs, 'tstat_one_sample.nii.gz')\np_volume = np.ones(ref.shape, dtype=np.float32)\np_volume[mask] = p_fwe\np_img = nib.Nifti1Image(p_volume, ref.affine, ref.header)\np_img.set_data_dtype(np.float32)\nnib.save(p_img, OUT/'p_fwe_maxT.nii.gz')\nlogp_img = save_map(-np.log10(np.maximum(p_fwe, 1/(N_PERM+1))), 'neglog10_p_fwe.nii.gz')\n\n# Independent atlas label supports the anatomical localization. Harvard-Oxford\n# cortical atlas, 25% max-probability; vmPFC operationalization: Frontal Medial\n# Cortex label restricted to y >= 0 and z <= 0 in MNI coordinates. This is an\n# explicit ventral medial subset, not a region used to select whole-brain results.\natlas = datasets.fetch_atlas_harvard_oxford('cort-maxprob-thr25-2mm')\natlas_img = image.resample_to_img(atlas.maps, ref, interpolation='nearest')\nlabels = [str(x.decode() if isinstance(x, bytes) else x) for x in atlas.labels]\nfrontal_idx = next(i for i, label in enumerate(labels) if label.lower() == 'frontal medial cortex')\natlas_dat = atlas_img.get_fdata().astype(int)\nijk = np.indices(ref.shape)\nxyz = nib.affines.apply_affine(ref.affine, np.moveaxis(ijk,0,-1))\nvm_mask = mask & (atlas_dat == frontal_idx) & (xyz[...,1] >= 0) & (xyz[...,2] <= 0)\nvm_idx = np.flatnonzero(vm_mask[mask])\nvm_fwe_sig = p_fwe[vm_idx] <= ALPHA\n\n# Label significant whole-brain voxels and identify overlap with anatomical ROI.\nsig = p_fwe <= ALPHA\nsig_vol = np.zeros(ref.shape, dtype=bool)\nsig_vol[mask] = sig\ncoords = np.column_stack(np.where(sig_vol))\nworld = nib.affines.apply_affine(ref.affine, coords) if len(coords) else np.empty((0,3))\ncluster_labels, n_clusters = label(sig_vol, structure=np.ones((3,3,3), dtype=int))\ncluster_rows = []\nfor cluster_id in range(1, n_clusters+1):\n    vox = np.column_stack(np.where(cluster_labels == cluster_id))\n    tv = t_img.get_fdata()[cluster_labels == cluster_id]\n    peak = vox[np.argmax(tv)]\n    peak_world = nib.affines.apply_affine(ref.affine, peak)\n    cluster_rows.append({'cluster':cluster_id,'voxels':len(vox),'peak_t':float(tv.max()),\n                         'peak_x':float(peak_world[0]),'peak_y':float(peak_world[1]),\n                         'peak_z':float(peak_world[2])})\npd.DataFrame(cluster_rows).to_csv(OUT/'fwe_clusters.tsv',sep='\\t',index=False)\nsig_roi = bool(np.any(vm_fwe_sig))\nroi_mean = float(np.mean(mean[vm_idx])) if len(vm_idx) else float('nan')\nroi_median_t = float(np.median(t_obs[vm_idx])) if len(vm_idx) else float('nan')\nroi_max_t = float(np.max(t_obs[vm_idx])) if len(vm_idx) else float('nan')\nroi_max_p = float(np.min(p_fwe[vm_idx])) if len(vm_idx) else float('nan')\nroi_mean_t = float(np.mean(t_obs[vm_idx])) if len(vm_idx) else float('nan')\n# An interpretable unthresholded ROI summary with t-based CI; descriptive, not the\n# inferential basis for hypothesis support.\nroi_data = arrays[:, vm_mask]\nsubject_roi = roi_data.mean(axis=1)\nroi_se = subject_roi.std(ddof=1)/np.sqrt(n)\nroi_ci = stats.t.interval(.95, df, loc=subject_roi.mean(), scale=roi_se)\n\nsummary = {\n 'n_participants':n, 'n_voxels_common_mask':int(v), 'mask_rule':'finite and nonzero in all 54 maps, intersected with resampled MNI152 2 mm brain mask',\n 'permutations':N_PERM, 'seed':SEED, 'test':'one-sided positive one-sample t; sign-flip max-T whole-brain FWE',\n 'minimum_attainable_p':1/(N_PERM+1), 'whole_brain_fwe_alpha':ALPHA,\n 'n_fwe_significant_voxels':int(sig.sum()), 'n_fwe_significant_clusters_26conn':int(n_clusters),\n 'n_positive_voxels_uncorrected_p_lt_05':int(np.sum((p_unc < .05) & (t_obs > 0))),\n 'max_t_whole_brain':float(np.max(t_obs)), 'min_p_fwe_whole_brain':float(np.min(p_fwe)),\n 'permutation_max_t_range':[float(max_t.min()),float(max_t.max())],\n 'significant_peak_mni_mm':(world[np.argmax(t_obs[np.flatnonzero(sig)])].round(1).tolist() if np.any(sig) else None),\n 'vmPFC_roi_definition':'Harvard-Oxford cortical Frontal Medial Cortex max-probability 25%, MNI y>=0 and z<=0; descriptive localization only',\n 'vmPFC_n_voxels':int(vm_mask.sum()), 'vmPFC_mean_across_voxels_effect':roi_mean,\n 'vmPFC_subject_mean_effect':float(subject_roi.mean()), 'vmPFC_subject_mean_95ci': [float(roi_ci[0]),float(roi_ci[1])],\n 'vmPFC_mean_t_across_voxels':roi_mean_t, 'vmPFC_median_voxel_t':roi_median_t,\n 'vmPFC_max_voxel_t':roi_max_t, 'vmPFC_min_voxel_p_fwe':roi_max_p,\n 'vmPFC_max_voxel_p_uncorrected':float(np.min(p_unc[vm_idx])) if len(vm_idx) else None,\n 'vmPFC_has_fwe_significant_voxel':sig_roi,\n 'all_subject_maps_finite':bool(np.isfinite(arrays).all()), 'excluded_participants':[],\n 'subject_mean_effect_range':[float(subject_roi.min()),float(subject_roi.max())],\n 'subject_global_sd_range':[float(np.min([q['common_mask_sd'] for q in qc])),float(np.max([q['common_mask_sd'] for q in qc]))]\n}\n(OUT/'summary.json').write_text(json.dumps(summary,indent=2))\npd.DataFrame({'voxelwise_t':t_obs,'p_uncorrected':p_unc,'p_fwe_maxT':p_fwe,'mean_effect':mean}).to_csv(OUT/'voxelwise_results_common_mask.tsv.gz',sep='\\t',index=False,compression='gzip')\n\n# Whole-brain visual: display unthresholded t and FWE significant voxels.\n# MNI template is only a background for localization.\ntemplate = datasets.load_mni152_template(resolution=2)\n# Positive-direction uncorrected display only. Whole-brain support is assessed\n# from the max-T family-wise corrected results above.\npos_unc_img = save_map(np.where(t_obs > stats.t.ppf(.95,df), t_obs, 0),\n                       'tstat_positive_uncorrected_p05.nii.gz')\nplotting.plot_stat_map(pos_unc_img, bg_img=template, threshold=0, display_mode='z',\n                       cut_coords=[-24,-12,0,12,24,36,48], cmap='hot',\n                       title='Positive gain effect: uncorrected p < .05 (display only)',\n                       colorbar=True, output_file=str(FIG/'whole_brain_t_uncorrected.png'))\n# Save corrected effects separately. An all-zero map means none pass.\nsig_img = save_map(np.where(sig, t_obs, 0), 'tstat_fwe_significant.nii.gz')\nplotting.plot_stat_map(sig_img, bg_img=template, threshold=0, display_mode='z',\n                       cut_coords=[-12,-8,-4,0,4,8,12], cmap='hot',\n                       title='Positive gain effect: whole-brain max-T FWE p ≤ .05',\n                       colorbar=True, output_file=str(FIG/'whole_brain_fwe.png'))\n\n# Anatomical ROI descriptive estimate plot; one participant is one point.\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7.2,4.6))\nax.axhline(0,color='0.5',lw=1)\nax.scatter(np.arange(1,n+1), subject_roi, s=24, alpha=.75, edgecolor='none')\nax.axhline(subject_roi.mean(),color='#b2182b',lw=2,label=f'Mean {subject_roi.mean():.3f}')\nax.fill_between([1,n], roi_ci[0],roi_ci[1],color='#b2182b',alpha=.14,label='95% t interval for mean')\nax.set(xlabel='Participant (sorted ID)',ylabel='Mean gain-effect estimate in vmPFC mask',\n       title='Participant-level vmPFC summaries (descriptive)')\nax.set_xlim(.5,n+.5)\nax.legend(frameon=False); fig.tight_layout(); fig.savefig(FIG/'vmPFC_subject_estimates.png',dpi=180); plt.close(fig)\nprint(json.dumps(summary,indent=2))\n{\n  \"n_participants\": 54,\n  \"n_voxels_common_mask\": 186400,\n  \"mask_rule\": \"finite and nonzero in all 54 maps, intersected with resampled MNI152 2 mm brain mask\",\n  \"permutations\": 10000,\n  \"seed\": 20261016,\n  \"test\": \"one-sided positive one-sample t; sign-flip max-T whole-brain FWE\",\n  \"minimum_attainable_p\": 9.999000099990002e-05,\n  \"whole_brain_fwe_alpha\": 0.05,\n  \"n_fwe_significant_voxels\": 23,\n  \"n_fwe_significant_clusters_26conn\": 3,\n  \"n_positive_voxels_uncorrected_p_lt_05\": 7326,\n  \"max_t_whole_brain\": 6.617173246302241,\n  \"min_p_fwe_whole_brain\": 0.0005999400059994001,\n  \"permutation_max_t_range\": [\n    3.292423963546753,\n    7.741138935089111\n  ],\n  \"significant_peak_mni_mm\": [\n    -26.0,\n    -102.0,\n    -3.6\n  ],\n  \"vmPFC_roi_definition\": \"Harvard-Oxford cortical Frontal Medial Cortex max-probability 25%, MNI y>=0 and z<=0; descriptive localization only\",\n  \"vmPFC_n_voxels\": 769,\n  \"vmPFC_mean_across_voxels_effect\": -0.009139063169808686,\n  \"vmPFC_subject_mean_effect\": -0.009139069356024265,\n  \"vmPFC_subject_mean_95ci\": [\n    -0.03989419796105427,\n    0.021616059249005743\n  ],\n  \"vmPFC_mean_t_across_voxels\": -0.38605776951083376,\n  \"vmPFC_median_voxel_t\": -0.3566124796393278,\n  \"vmPFC_max_voxel_t\": 2.4514919045595347,\n  \"vmPFC_min_voxel_p_fwe\": 1.0,\n  \"vmPFC_max_voxel_p_uncorrected\": 0.008778317690051808,\n  \"vmPFC_has_fwe_significant_voxel\": false,\n  \"all_subject_maps_finite\": true,\n  \"excluded_participants\": [],\n  \"subject_mean_effect_range\": [\n    -0.2589581310749054,\n    0.27372363209724426\n  ],\n  \"subject_global_sd_range\": [\n    0.08515932410955429,\n    0.26501762866973877\n  ]\n}participant_id\tage\tgender\tnonzero_voxels\tcommon_mask_mean\tcommon_mask_sd\tcommon_mask_min\tcommon_mask_max\tn_nonfinite\nsub-001\t24\tM\t225441\t-0.04559144005179405\t0.14764858782291412\t-0.9509850740432739\t1.72283136844635\t0\nsub-00