{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc '\"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response items > \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT/commands/response-items.stdout\" 2> \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT/commands/response-items.stderr\"; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response template --help; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response stamp --help; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response set --help; find \"$(dirname \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\")/..\" -maxdepth 2 -name judge-target.json -print; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT/commands/response-items.stdout\"'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc '\"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response items > \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT/commands/response-items.stdout\" 2> \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT/commands/response-items.stderr\"; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response template --help; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response stamp --help; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response set --help; find \"$(dirname \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\")/..\" -maxdepth 2 -name judge-target.json -print; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT/commands/response-items.stdout\"'","aggregated_output":"✔ The default environment has been installed.\nusage: encode_response.py template [-h] [-o OUTPUT]\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help           show this help message and exit\n  -o, --output OUTPUT\n✔ The default environment has been installed.\nusage: encode_response.py stamp [-h] [--harness HARNESS] [--model MODEL]\n                                [--scope SCOPE]\n                                [--kind {dataset,component,study,skill}]\n                                [--dependency DEPENDENCY] [--capture CAPTURE]\n                                response\n\npositional arguments:\n  response\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n  --harness HARNESS     e.g. \"claude-code (subagent)\"\n  --model MODEL\n  --scope SCOPE         what this assessment did and did not cover\n  --kind {dataset,component,study,skill}\n                        the object's kind (see references/project-kinds.md)\n  --dependency DEPENDENCY\n                        e.g. \"stamped-conventions@0.1.0\"; repeatable\n  --capture CAPTURE     hash a retained delivery manifest/archive; preserve\n                        any recorded Git commit\n✔ The default environment has been installed.\nusage: encode_response.py set [-h] --item ITEM\n                              --status {met,not-applicable,not-checked,partial,unmet}\n                              --reason REASON [--source SOURCE]\n                              [--evidence EVIDENCE] [--flag FLAGS]\n                              [--drop-evidence]\n                              response\n\npositional arguments:\n  response\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n  --item ITEM           item id, with or without the 'stamped-checklist:'\n                        prefix\n  --status {met,not-applicable,not-checked,partial,unmet}\n  --reason REASON\n  --source SOURCE       probe:<name>, judgement, probe:<name>+judgement,\n                        human:web-app\n  --evidence EVIDENCE   uri, or uri=description; repeatable\n  --flag FLAGS\n  --drop-evidence       clear the evidence already on this item (by default it\n                        is kept when --evidence is not given)\nstamped-checklist:must/001\tmust\tS.1\tAre all files and directories nested under a common root?\nstamped-checklist:must/002\tmust\tS.1\tAre datasets included, linked, or referenced reachable from the code and environment specifications without crossing the boundary of a common root?\nstamped-checklist:must/003\tmust\tS.1\tIs external software included in the environment or linked as submodules within the project boundary?\nstamped-checklist:must/004\tmust\tT.1,T.3,T.5\tAre version control systems such as `Git` used for code, text, documentation, and configuration files?\nstamped-checklist:must/005\tmust\tT.1,T.3,T.5\tAre version control systems such as `git-annex`, DataLad, or `Git` LFS used for large binary data?\nstamped-checklist:must/006\tmust\tT.1,T.3,T.5\tAre the exact environment specifications used to generate a set of results included in the provenance records to link computational actions to particular environments?\nstamped-checklist:must/007\tmust\tA.1\tIs a `README.md` or `Makefile` included with instructions for installation and usage?\nstamped-checklist:must/008\tmust\tA.1\tIs there a clear starting point for users to start reproducing results (e.g., a main script, a workflow definition, or a container image)?\nstamped-checklist:must/009\tmust\tP.1\tAre relative paths used in scripts, avoiding hardcoded or system-specific paths like `C:\\Users\\...` or `/home/user/...`?\nstamped-checklist:must/010\tmust\tP.1\tAre all software dependencies included in the environment specification, rather than relying on pre-installed tools?\nstamped-checklist:must/011\tmust\tP.1\tHave all assumptions about the host system's configuration been documented (e.g., specific OS versions, required system libraries, or environment variables)?\nstamped-checklist:must/012\tmust\tP.2\tIs there a clear list of system requirements and dependencies documented in the README or environment specifications?\nstamped-checklist:must/013\tmust\tP.3\tAre environment specifications (e.g., Dockerfiles, `pyproject.toml`, `package.json`) included in the version control system alongside code and data?\nstamped-checklist:must/014\tmust\tP.3\tIs there a process for updating environment specifications when dependencies change, and are these updates tracked in version control?\nstamped-checklist:must/015\tmust\tD.1\tAre environment specifications (e.g., container digests, frozen package manifests) included to ensure others can attempt to exactly replicate the environment?\nstamped-checklist:must/016\tmust\tD.1\tAre environment specifications shared in a way that others can access and use them (e.g., published container images, archived environment files)?\nstamped-checklist:must/017\tmust\tD.1\tIs there documentation on how to obtain and use the environment specifications for reproduction?\nstamped-checklist:should/001\tshould\tT.2\tIs a common version control system (e.g., `Git`, DVC, `git-annex`, or DataLad) used across all components?\nstamped-checklist:should/002\tshould\tA.2\tIs the workflow tested regularly to ensure instructions remain accurate?\nstamped-checklist:should/003\tshould\tM.1\tAre raw data, processed data, code, and environment definitions separated into distinct modules?\nstamped-checklist:should/009\tshould\tM.4\tAre restricted data or software kept in their own modules?\nstamped-checklist:should/004\tshould\tE.1\tIs the pipeline tested in a fresh container, batch job, clean virtual machine, or cloud-based instance?\nstamped-checklist:should/005\tshould\tE.1\tDoes the workflow spawn disposable environments for each execution, allowing for isolation of runs?\nstamped-checklist:should/006\tshould\tD.2\tAre the exact environment specifications used to generate a specific set of results regularly tested to ensure they can be reconstituted as intended?\nstamped-checklist:should/007\tshould\tD.2\tAre environment artifacts archived within the research object where possible (e.g., executable binaries, container images)?\nstamped-checklist:should/008\tshould\tD.2\tIs there a process for updating environment specifications when dependencies change, and are these updates tracked in version control?\nstamped-checklist:may/001\tmay\tM.2\tAre external datasets or software dependencies included as submodules or linked as submodules?\nstamped-checklist:may/002\tmay\tM.2\tIs the modular structure documented to clarify how components relate and can be recombined?\nstamped-checklist:may/003\tmay\tM.2\tAre modular boundaries defined in a way that allows independent updates without breaking the overall workflow?\nstamped-checklist:may/004\tmay\tM.2\tIs there a clear mechanism for composing modules together (e.g., `git` submodules, or container orchestration)?\nstamped-checklist:may/005\tmay\tM.2\tIs there documentation or tooling to support users in understanding and navigating the modular structure?\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'find \"$(dirname \"$(dirname \"$(dirname \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\")\")\")\" -maxdepth 2 -type f -print; python - \"$STAMPED_DELIVERY_MANIFEST\" <<'\"'PY'\nimport json,sys\nx=json.load(open(sys.argv[1])); print('policy=',x.get('policy')); print('git_dependencies=',json.dumps(x.get('git_dependencies'),indent=2)); print('omissions=',x.get('omissions'))\nprint('capture_sha256 from stop receipt is in receipt; HEAD=',x.get('head_commit'))\nPY\ncat \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/study-task.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/sources.json\"; git -C \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\" remote -v; git -C \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\" config --local --list --show-origin; git -C \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\" ls-files -s | head -80; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/.git/config\"'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'find \"$(dirname \"$(dirname \"$(dirname \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\")\")\")\" -maxdepth 2 -type f -print; python - \"$STAMPED_DELIVERY_MANIFEST\" <<'\"'PY'\nimport json,sys\nx=json.load(open(sys.argv[1])); print('policy=',x.get('policy')); print('git_dependencies=',json.dumps(x.get('git_dependencies'),indent=2)); print('omissions=',x.get('omissions'))\nprint('capture_sha256 from stop receipt is in receipt; HEAD=',x.get('head_commit'))\nPY\ncat \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/study-task.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/sources.json\"; git -C \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\" remote -v; git -C \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\" config --local --list --show-origin; git -C \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\" ls-files -s | head -80; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/.git/config\"'","aggregated_output":"/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/assessment-series/narps-neutral-r1-operational-v4/targets/narps-neutral-r1-23-j01/canonical/manifest.json\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/assessment-series/narps-neutral-r1-operational-v4/targets/narps-neutral-r1-23-j01/operational/manifest.json\npolicy= {'credentials': 'excluded with reason', 'environment_and_cache_bytes': 'included', 'ignored_files': 'included', 'symlinks': 'preserved without following'}\ngit_dependencies= []\nomissions= []\ncapture_sha256 from stop receipt is in receipt; HEAD= afb62884c70115b6e46a92ddcd1d17bbf4f33544\n# Potential gains and brain activity\n\nDoes increasing potential monetary gain produce a greater fMRI response in the ventromedial prefrontal cortex (vmPFC) in the NARPS equal-indifference group?\n\nTest the hypothesis that this response increases with potential gain.\nParticipants performed a mixed-gambles task, deciding whether to accept gambles with possible gains and losses.\nIn this group, the range of potential gains was twice the range of potential losses.\n\nInvestigate the question at the group-analysis stage using the supplied 54 participant gain-effect maps and metadata.\nPreprocessing and individual modeling are fixed inputs, described in `upstream-methods.md`; do not repeat them or download time-series scans.\nChoose scientifically defensible group models, quality checks, software and visualizations.\nAccount for what the supplied effect estimates measure and the limits of their upstream model.\nUse whole-brain correction for multiple comparisons when evaluating support for the hypothesis, as required by the original study; justify how you identify vmPFC.\n\nProduce a concise scientific report with informative figures and the code and results supporting your conclusions.\nExplain uncertainty, exclusions and limitations, including what a lack of support would mean.\nPrior teams' answers are not ground truth or a required result.\n\nSource: [NARPS original analysis instructions, Hypothesis 1](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf).\n{\n  \"study\": \"NARPS Hypothesis 1: group inference from fixed participant effects\",\n  \"collection\": \"https://neurovault.org/collections/4932/\",\n  \"team\": \"AO86\",\n  \"image_metadata_api\": \"https://neurovault.org/api/collections/4932/images/?limit=2000\",\n  \"selection\": \"All single-subject unthresholded hypo1 cope images; participant IDs exactly match the 54 equalIndifference participants in the pinned source table.\",\n  \"maps\": 54,\n  \"map_bytes\": 96387264,\n  \"map_type\": \"participant contrast parameter estimate, not a z/t statistic or a group result\",\n  \"spatial_reference\": \"MNI152NLin2009cAsym\",\n  \"upstream_processing_fixed\": true,\n  \"cohort_source\": {\n    \"url\": \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OpenNeuroDatasets/ds001734/64ec117560ab58ed0fe1273f3e90887b1768b268/participants.tsv\",\n    \"git_commit\": \"64ec117560ab58ed0fe1273f3e90887b1768b268\",\n    \"sha256\": \"d127d700f9a519338da295ea56455bf7bb46ca572f726da72ef7b57c310687b8\",\n    \"transformation\": \"Select group == equalIndifference; preserve all columns and values.\"\n  },\n  \"methods_source\": {\n    \"url\": \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/poldrack/narps/0a9d71a5ab435b40dcd50fea82d2961a43f0a3fc/ImageAnalyses/metadata_files/analysis_pipelines_for_analysis.xlsx\",\n    \"git_commit\": \"0a9d71a5ab435b40dcd50fea82d2961a43f0a3fc\",\n    \"sha256\": \"bfb66119c6beb26e5d3fc84638435cf6c49b40e5eb06606bfecaee0aa48d82b7\",\n    \"sheet\": \"Sheet1\",\n    \"header_excel_row\": 2,\n    \"team_id\": \"AO86\"\n  },\n  \"checksum_scope\": \"SHA256 values are computed from retrieved bytes; NeuroVault metadata independently supplies file sizes but no source checksums.\",\n  \"files\": {\n    \"data/sub-001_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\": {\n      \"url\": \"https://neurovault.org/media/images/4932/sub-001_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\",\n      \"image_id\": 118651,\n      \"participant_id\": \"sub-001\",\n      \"bytes\": 1794522,\n      \"sha256\": \"baf3440fbc7f5f59dea28d231e9c5eb9cf00a15b6924342f4b79e7faf56a929a\",\n      \"transformation\": \"none\"\n    },\n    \"data/sub-003_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\": {\n      \"url\": \"https://neurovault.org/media/images/4932/sub-003_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\",\n      \"image_id\": 118661,\n      \"participant_id\": \"sub-003\",\n      \"bytes\": 1762018,\n      \"sha256\": \"a8c9b6276d1597bc43695516c9ac5b2893a8d8408cd053161ceaddbd42dafb20\",\n      \"transformation\": \"none\"\n    },\n    \"data/sub-005_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\": {\n      \"url\": \"https://neurovault.org/media/images/4932/sub-005_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\",\n      \"image_id\": 118671,\n      \"participant_id\": \"sub-005\",\n      \"bytes\": 1847315,\n      \"sha256\": \"415383874c65fd9e10bb03bf2aa59bfcd1d10650bc5d0560a249e85d52b7b205\",\n      \"transformation\": \"none\"\n    },\n    \"data/sub-009_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\": {\n      \"url\": \"https://neurovault.org/media/images/4932/sub-009_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\",\n      \"image_id\": 118686,\n 